ds:concept.How-to-support-modifiers-in-a-component a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "How to support modifiers in a component";
    ds:summary "This guide covers the steps to make a component follow modifiers: identifying its colour properties, adding the modifier channel and surface fallbacks, adding interaction state tokens, and testing across modifier classes and themes.";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "**Status:** Final  \n**Version:** 1.0  \n**Last updated:** 2026-09-11  \n**Source:** `@canonical/react-tokens` Storybook guide — _How to support modifiers in a component_\n\n\nThis guide shows you how to make a component respond to modifier classes (`.error`, `.success`, `.warning`, etc.) through the token channel variable system. No JavaScript is involved.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n- A working React component with CSS\n- `@canonical/styles` imported in your project\n- Familiarity with CSS custom properties\n\n## Steps\n\n### 1. Identify the color properties in your component\n\nList every CSS property that sets a color. These are the properties that need modifier support:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n/* Before: direct semantic references */\n.ds.my-component {\n  color: var(--color-text);\n  border-color: var(--color-border);\n  background: var(--color-foreground-ghost);\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n### 2. Add the modifier channel fallback\n\nFor each color property, prepend the matching `--modifier-*` channel variable:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n/* After: modifier-aware */\n.ds.my-component {\n  color: var(--modifier-color-text, var(--color-text));\n  border-color: var(--modifier-color-border, var(--color-border));\n  background: var(--modifier-color-foreground-ghost, var(--color-foreground-ghost));\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nThe channel variable names follow a consistent pattern: `--modifier-color-{rest}` where `{rest}` matches the base semantic token name after `--color-`.\n\n| Base semantic token | Modifier channel |\n|--------------------|-----------------|\n| `--color-text` | `--modifier-color-text` |\n| `--color-border` | `--modifier-color-border` |\n| `--color-icon-default` | `--modifier-color-icon` |\n| `--color-foreground-primary` | `--modifier-color-foreground-primary` |\n| `--color-foreground-ghost` | `--modifier-color-foreground-ghost` |\n| `--color-foreground-input` | `--modifier-color-foreground-input` |\n| `--color-focus-ring` | `--modifier-color-focus-ring` |\n\n### 3. Add surface fallbacks (if applicable)\n\nIf the component renders a background or sits inside layered containers, add the surface channel between modifier and base:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n.ds.my-component {\n  background: var(\n    --modifier-color-foreground-ghost,\n    var(--surface-color-foreground-ghost,\n      var(--color-foreground-ghost))\n  );\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nFor the component's own background when it acts as a surface:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n.ds.my-component {\n  background: var(--surface-color-background, var(--color-background));\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n### 4. Add interaction state tokens (if interactive)\n\nFor hover, active, and disabled states on interactive elements:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n.ds.my-component:hover {\n  background: var(\n    --hover--color-foreground-primary,\n    var(--modifier-color-foreground-primary,\n      var(--color-foreground-primary))\n  );\n}\n\n.ds.my-component:active {\n  background: var(\n    --active--color-foreground-primary,\n    var(--modifier-color-foreground-primary,\n      var(--color-foreground-primary))\n  );\n}\n\n.ds.my-component:disabled {\n  background: var(\n    --disabled--color-foreground-primary,\n    var(--modifier-color-foreground-primary,\n      var(--color-foreground-primary))\n  );\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n### 5. Test with modifier classes\n\nWrap your component in modifier containers to verify:\n\n\\`\\`\\`html\n<!-- Default (no modifier) -->\n<MyComponent />\n\n<!-- Error context -->\n<div class=\"error\">\n  <MyComponent />\n</div>\n\n<!-- Success context -->\n<div class=\"success\">\n  <MyComponent />\n</div>\n\n<!-- Warning context -->\n<div class=\"warning\">\n  <MyComponent />\n</div>\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nIn Storybook, the **Modifiers story** demonstrates this pattern with live token tables.\n\n### 6. Test across themes\n\nSwitch the Storybook theme between light and dark. All modifier colors should remain legible because the modifier-specific semantic tokens (like `--color-text-error`) carry paired light/dark values.\n\n## Variations\n\n### Components that only need text/icon color\n\nSimple components like labels or badges may only need the text channel:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n.ds.label {\n  color: var(--modifier-color-text, var(--color-text));\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n### Components with multiple color zones\n\nComplex components like cards may have a header with a background and a body with text. Apply the appropriate channel to each zone:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n.ds.card {\n  & > .header {\n    background: var(--modifier-color-foreground-primary, var(--color-foreground-primary));\n    color: var(--color-text-on-foreground);\n  }\n\n  & > .body {\n    color: var(--modifier-color-text, var(--color-text));\n    border-top-color: var(--modifier-color-border, var(--color-border-subtle));\n  }\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n> **Note**: `--color-text-on-foreground` does not have a modifier channel because it is always read on top of a foreground surface. Its value adapts via theme pairing, not modifier rebinding.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n### Colors don't change inside `.error`\n\nVerify you are using the modifier channel `var(--modifier-color-text, ...)` and not a direct `var(--color-text)` reference. The modifier class only sets `--modifier-*` variables — it cannot intercept a direct semantic reference.\n\n### Colors look wrong in nested modifiers\n\nIf a component is inside both `.error` and an inner `.success`, the inner modifier wins because CSS custom properties inherit to the closest ancestor. This is expected behavior. If you need to prevent nesting, that is a layout concern, not a token concern.\n\n### Focus ring doesn't change\n\nUse `--modifier-color-focus-ring` for the focus ring color:\n\n\\`\\`\\`css\n.ds.input:focus-visible {\n  outline-color: var(--modifier-color-focus-ring, var(--color-focus-ring));\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\n## Related\n\n- **How to find the right token** — token selection guide\n- **The Modifier Contract** — understanding why channels exist\n- **How to handle token changes** — managing pre-1.0 token changes";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.How-to-guide.
